---
servings: 4
title: Spinach chana dal
tags: vegetarian, gluten-free, simmered
image: https://images.immediate.co.uk/production/volatile/sites/30/2026/05/Screenshot-2026-08-11-at-15.00.43-ff34ac4.png?resize=814,1068
source: https://www.bbcgoodfood.com/recipes/spinach-chana-dal
prep time: 15m
cook time: 50m
---

Soak the @chana lentil{200%g}(Bengal gram) for at least ~{2%hours} or overnight. Rinse them and put in a #saucepan{} with 1 litre of cold water and the @bicarbonate of soda{0.25%tsp}. Bring to the boil, remove the arising foam until the surface is clear, then mix in the @ground turmeric{1%tsp}.

Bubble gently for ~{20%minutes} until you can squash a lentil between your fingers easily. Add the @tomato{1}(roughly chopped) and the @frozen spinach{150%g}(defrosted). Cook for another ~{15%minutes}, stirring regularly and mashing the lentils against the side of the #saucepan{} to thicken the dal. Add a splash of hot water, if it's getting a little dry. Mix in salt to taste. Will keep frozen for up to three months, see tip.

Then, make the tadka. In a small #frying pan{}, melt the @ghee{1%tbsp}(solid) over a medium-high heat until it melts. Add the @dry red chilli{2}(small) and sizzle for a few seconds, then add the @cumin seed{1%tsp}, @garlic clove{3}(peeled and sliced) and @chilli powder{0.25%tsp}. You'll need to work quickly so the spices don't burn. Turn off the heat and pour the tadka over the cooked lentils. My granny's tip is to spoon a little dal back into the #frying pan{} to nab any tadka left behind, before tipping it back into the dish. Serve with warm naans.
